// The page heap, Go's mheap without its spans: the one place the runtime takes memory from the system and gives it back,
// shared by every thread, under a lock. It hands out runs of whole pages: the chunks that the heaps of the threads carve
// their size classes from, the blocks of large boxes, the stacks of the classes, and the blocks the runtime keeps. A run
// that comes back is free for the next at once, its pages still committed, and is merged with the free pages beside it
// for nothing, since the free pages are a bitmap, not lists.
//
// Its memory is arenas, address space reserved from the system arenaBytes at a time (a run longer than that gets an arena
// of its own size), each with a header in its first pages: the arena's place in the list, its size, where a search starts,
// and four bitmaps of a bit per page: the pages given out, those written since the system last zeroed them (dirty), those
// the system holds no memory for (released: never committed, or given back), and those freed since the last release pass
// (recent). A run is the lowest one of the first arena that has room, as Go's page allocator finds the lowest address.
// Pages that are released are zero when the run takes them, and so are the pages of an arena never used; only the dirty
// pages of a run are zeroed, and only the bytes of them that the caller asks for, as Go's needzero spares memory fresh
// from the system, and growslice clears only the tail that its copy does not write.
//
// The memory of free pages goes back to the system only by a release pass, Go's scavenger in its simplest sound form: at
// most once a scavengePeriodNanos, from a free into the page heap and from the monitor's round, a pass gives back the free
// pages that were free at the pass before as well, from the highest page down, until the committed free pages are no more
// than retainDivisor-th of the pages in use, Go's retainExtraPercent. A program that keeps freeing and allocating keeps
// its free pages and faults none in again; one that goes idle gives them back within two periods, down to a tenth of what
// it holds; a program without the scheduler has no monitor, so its pass runs only at its frees. What a release costs, the
// system's call, is paid under the lock, once a period at most.
//
// What it leaves out of Go's: the spans, mcentral and the sweep, which serve a collector that this runtime has not; the
// radix tree of summaries over the bitmaps (a search walks an arena's bitmap a word at a time, which a heap of a few arenas
// does not need more than); the per-processor page cache, whose part the heaps' kept large blocks play (heap.maxon); the
// background scavenger's pacing to 1% of the processors' time and its huge-page awareness; and the arena index, since a
// free finds its arena by walking the few there are. An arena is never unmapped, as Go never unmaps one.

// pageBytes is the page heap's page, Go's (_PageSize): every run is a whole number of them, so a run is aligned to it.
let pageBytes = 8192 as Count

let pageShift = 13 as Count

let pageMask = 8191 as Count

// arenaPages is how many pages the page heap reserves at once, Go's heapArenaBytes on 64-bit Linux, 64 MB: address space,
// which costs nothing until a run commits it.
let arenaPages = 8192 as Count

// releasePages is the run that a release gives back at least, aligned to it in its arena: 16 KB, the page of macOS on
// arm64, the largest of the systems', which a release must not split.
let releasePages = 2 as Count

// retainDivisor keeps the committed free pages a release pass leaves at a tenth of those in use, Go's retainExtraPercent:
// room to allocate from without faulting pages in.
let retainDivisor = 10 as Count

// scavengePeriodNanos is how often a release pass runs at most, and how long a free page stays free before one gives it
// back.
let scavengePeriodNanos = 1000000000 as Count

// The bitmaps of an arena, in this order after its header: a bit per page, the bit of a page's index in the word at its
// index over pagesPerBitmapWord.
let bitmapUsed = 0 as Count

let bitmapDirty = 1 as Count

let bitmapReleased = 2 as Count

let bitmapRecent = 3 as Count

let arenaBitmaps = 4 as Count

let pagesPerBitmapWord = 64 as Count

let bitmapWordShift = 6 as Count

let bitmapBitMask = 63 as Count

let allBits = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as Count

let lowestBit = 1 as Count

// bitsPerByte turns an arena's pages into the bytes of one of its bitmaps.
let bitsPerByteShift = 3 as Count

// noPage is no page of an arena: the first page holds the header, so no run starts there.
let noPage = 0 as Count

let noArena = 0 as Count

// pagesFor is how many pages hold a number of bytes.
function pagesFor(bytes Count) returns Count
	let rounded = try bytes + pageMask otherwise panic("out of memory: a block past the address space")
	return rounded shr pageShift
end 'pagesFor'

// pageAlloc is the address of a new run of count pages of the page heap whose first arena is pages, with its bytes from
// zeroFrom to zeroTo zeroed and the others as the pages held them. The search and the bitmaps are under the lock; the
// zeroing is not, since the run is the caller's once it is marked.
function pageAlloc(pages Count, count Count, zeroFrom Count, zeroTo Count) returns Count
